As shown in the figure, a slope with length $s=5\mathrm{m}$ and inclination angle $\theta=37^{\circ}$ is smoothly connected to a horizontal conveyor belt and the horizontal ground through a small smooth arc. The conveyor belt has a length $L=1.6\mathrm{m}$ and runs counterclockwise at a constant speed $v_{0}=4\mathrm{m}/\mathrm{s}$. A block, treated as a point mass, is gently placed at the right end $A$ of the conveyor belt. When the block slides to the left end $B$ of the conveyor belt, it reaches the same speed as the conveyor belt and then slides down the slope. It is known that the coefficient of kinetic friction $\mu$ between the block and the conveyor belt, the slope, and the horizontal ground is the same. The block eventually comes to rest at point $D$ on the horizontal ground. Given $g=10\mathrm{m}/\mathrm{s}^{2}$, $\sin 37^{\circ}=0.6$, and $\cos 37^{\circ}=0.8$, find:

1. the coefficient of kinetic friction $\mu$;
2. the magnitude of the velocity of the block when it slides to point $C$;
3. the time taken for the block to travel from $A$ to $D$.
